WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.284 --> 00:00:01.950
ROBERT KUBIS: In this
episode of "Things

00:00:01.950 --> 00:00:03.366
I Learned About
Cloud Spanner," we

00:00:03.366 --> 00:00:05.290
look at choosing the
right primary keys

00:00:05.290 --> 00:00:09.220
to help detect and avoid
traffic jams during data load.

00:00:09.220 --> 00:00:11.730
Will we be able to clear
the data highway congestion?

00:00:11.730 --> 00:00:13.000
Stay tuned to find out.

00:00:16.720 --> 00:00:18.670
So a while ago, my
friend Anna approached

00:00:18.670 --> 00:00:22.000
me asking if I could give her
advice on a database migration.

00:00:22.000 --> 00:00:23.710
Her online shop,
Pile of Gadgets,

00:00:23.710 --> 00:00:26.620
selling toys like the Cloud
Spanner fidget spinner,

00:00:26.620 --> 00:00:28.620
has grown tremendously,
and she was

00:00:28.620 --> 00:00:30.370
worried to have the
business running

00:00:30.370 --> 00:00:33.310
on the back of a traditional
relational database.

00:00:33.310 --> 00:00:35.230
After watching the
first episode of "Things

00:00:35.230 --> 00:00:36.940
I Learned About Cloud
Spanner," her team

00:00:36.940 --> 00:00:40.690
decided to explore and
evaluate this new SQL database.

00:00:40.690 --> 00:00:43.150
They started from a recent
backup of the database

00:00:43.150 --> 00:00:45.910
where all the data was
exported through CSV files

00:00:45.910 --> 00:00:48.040
and uploaded to
Google Cloud Storage.

00:00:48.040 --> 00:00:49.720
They then wrote a
little loader program

00:00:49.720 --> 00:00:52.480
that was reading the files,
processing them, and writing

00:00:52.480 --> 00:00:54.100
the data to Cloud Spanner.

00:00:54.100 --> 00:00:55.420
So far so good.

00:00:55.420 --> 00:00:57.430
Having the files in
Google Cloud Storage

00:00:57.430 --> 00:00:59.770
and processing them
from a GCE instance,

00:00:59.770 --> 00:01:02.770
Cloud Storage or Cloud Spanner
instance, is actually great.

00:01:02.770 --> 00:01:06.090
This way you don't need to worry
about network capacity or added

00:01:06.090 --> 00:01:08.980
latencies being an issue
during your data load.

00:01:08.980 --> 00:01:11.290
However, while running
their first data loads

00:01:11.290 --> 00:01:14.860
on a regional instance, there
was some disappointment.

00:01:14.860 --> 00:01:19.430
They only saw a 35% utilization
of their three node instance.

00:01:19.430 --> 00:01:21.760
Trying to increase the
utilization by scaling

00:01:21.760 --> 00:01:24.790
out the loaders
didn't help either.

00:01:24.790 --> 00:01:26.210
Now, that was interesting.

00:01:26.210 --> 00:01:29.590
It looked like their load is
hot spotting on only one node.

00:01:29.590 --> 00:01:32.860
Three nodes, 35%--
you do the math.

00:01:32.860 --> 00:01:36.100
To verify the assumption,
they scaled the instance

00:01:36.100 --> 00:01:38.950
to five nodes and saw that
the utilization dropped

00:01:38.950 --> 00:01:42.940
to around 20%, while the number
of transactions and throughputs

00:01:42.940 --> 00:01:44.590
stayed the same.

00:01:44.590 --> 00:01:47.650
And that was a clear indicator
that only one of the nodes

00:01:47.650 --> 00:01:49.360
was doing work here
while the others--

00:01:49.360 --> 00:01:50.860
[SPINNING]

00:01:50.860 --> 00:01:53.260
At this point, we needed
to dig a little deeper

00:01:53.260 --> 00:01:55.570
and understand how
Cloud Spanner organizes

00:01:55.570 --> 00:01:57.850
data to scale your workloads.

00:01:57.850 --> 00:01:59.800
Reading the
documentation, you find

00:01:59.800 --> 00:02:01.840
that Cloud Spanner
organizes rows

00:02:01.840 --> 00:02:05.920
in a table lexicographically,
or for the mere mortals like me,

00:02:05.920 --> 00:02:08.660
alphabetically by
the primary key.

00:02:08.660 --> 00:02:12.100
Then driven by the size of
your tables and load patterns,

00:02:12.100 --> 00:02:15.880
your data gets divided into
chunks, so-called splits.

00:02:15.880 --> 00:02:18.230
These splits are
distinct ranges of rows

00:02:18.230 --> 00:02:20.350
based on your primary key.

00:02:20.350 --> 00:02:23.290
To efficiently scale out
all your reads and writes,

00:02:23.290 --> 00:02:25.570
each individual
split can move freely

00:02:25.570 --> 00:02:28.950
between the available compute
nodes while making sure there

00:02:28.950 --> 00:02:29.890
is always--

00:02:29.890 --> 00:02:31.480
and I mean always--

00:02:31.480 --> 00:02:35.440
only one node that controls
the writes to a split.

00:02:35.440 --> 00:02:37.240
Now, that's where
it gets tricky.

00:02:37.240 --> 00:02:40.360
If you write to a table with
monotonically increasing

00:02:40.360 --> 00:02:45.190
or decreasing primary key
values, like 123 or ABC,

00:02:45.190 --> 00:02:48.250
all your requests get sent
to one split, the first

00:02:48.250 --> 00:02:50.050
or the last in your keyspace.

00:02:50.050 --> 00:02:51.880
And if you load
many rows at once,

00:02:51.880 --> 00:02:53.830
you eventually
exhaust the resources

00:02:53.830 --> 00:02:57.580
of your responsible
node for that one split.

00:02:57.580 --> 00:02:59.260
Packed with this newly
gained knowledge,

00:02:59.260 --> 00:03:01.780
we went back and took a
look at the schema and data

00:03:01.780 --> 00:03:03.350
from Pile of Gadget.

00:03:03.350 --> 00:03:05.590
It turned out that
the application uses

00:03:05.590 --> 00:03:09.280
an outer incremented integer
for some of their primary keys,

00:03:09.280 --> 00:03:12.670
and the data was exported
in that order, which

00:03:12.670 --> 00:03:14.580
is creating a
hotspot during load

00:03:14.580 --> 00:03:17.860
by adding new rows
always to the last split.

00:03:17.860 --> 00:03:20.470
To resolve this,
they needed to modify

00:03:20.470 --> 00:03:24.040
the schema, such as introducing
a distribution key that you

00:03:24.040 --> 00:03:27.880
prefix in your primary key, or
using a well distributed hash

00:03:27.880 --> 00:03:30.190
function to hash
your existing key,

00:03:30.190 --> 00:03:33.400
or introduce an
entirely new primary key

00:03:33.400 --> 00:03:35.550
that is well disputed.

00:03:35.550 --> 00:03:38.140
With future scalability
requirements in mind,

00:03:38.140 --> 00:03:40.690
they opted to
generate a new UUID

00:03:40.690 --> 00:03:44.320
version for base primary key
and kept the old ID around

00:03:44.320 --> 00:03:47.020
for potential legacy purposes.

00:03:47.020 --> 00:03:50.080
After these modifications,
they ran the data loads again

00:03:50.080 --> 00:03:52.570
and saw over time
a full utilization

00:03:52.570 --> 00:03:54.470
of their Cloud
Spanner instance where

00:03:54.470 --> 00:03:58.030
the throughput scaled with
the number of instance nodes.

00:03:58.030 --> 00:04:00.430
A little side note
here, as you start

00:04:00.430 --> 00:04:03.310
to load an empty database,
it will take some time

00:04:03.310 --> 00:04:06.670
for the first splits to occur
and the load-based rebalancing

00:04:06.670 --> 00:04:07.780
to kick in.

00:04:07.780 --> 00:04:11.230
So it will take some time before
you see the full utilization

00:04:11.230 --> 00:04:13.680
of your instance.

00:04:13.680 --> 00:04:15.950
So now my friend's
company, Pile of Gadget,

00:04:15.950 --> 00:04:18.290
was happy to continue
their evaluation

00:04:18.290 --> 00:04:20.779
and see if they could improve
the load performance even

00:04:20.779 --> 00:04:21.959
further.

00:04:21.959 --> 00:04:24.840
Unfortunately, we
are out of time.

00:04:24.840 --> 00:04:27.960
To find out what we did
next to maximize throughput

00:04:27.960 --> 00:04:30.180
during load, check
out the next episode

00:04:30.180 --> 00:04:32.520
of "Things I Learned
About Cloud Spanner."

00:04:32.520 --> 00:04:36.030
And remember, choosing well
distributed primary keys

00:04:36.030 --> 00:04:39.250
sets you up for scalability.

00:04:39.250 --> 00:04:40.990
Last but not least,
for the ones that

00:04:40.990 --> 00:04:43.030
love to dive into
source code and run

00:04:43.030 --> 00:04:45.220
the experiments, to
learn more, check out

00:04:45.220 --> 00:04:47.550
the links in the descriptions.

00:04:47.550 --> 00:04:50.900
[MUSIC PLAYING]

